  "worked_solution": "Let $W$ be the event that a randomly selected cap is white, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cap is from plant $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cap is from plant $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cap is from plant $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white, we can use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W\\cap A)+P(W\\cap B)+P(W\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since plant $A$ produces $36%$ of all the caps, plant $B$ produces $34%$ of all the caps, and plant $C$ produces $30%$ of all the caps, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.36,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of the caps from plant $A$ and $40%$ of the caps from plant $B$ are white, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|A)=0.25,\\,P(W|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(W)=0.46.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.46 & =(0.25)(0.36)+(0.4)(0.34)+P(W|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.46 & =0.09+0.136+0.3P(W|C) \\\\ 0.3P(W|C) & =0.234 \\\\ P(W|C) & =0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white is $0.78.$",
